

### Theorems on Continued Fractions Definitions

If the number of terms is finite, \(f\) is called a terminating continued fraction. If the number of terms 'is infinite, \(f\) is called an infinite continued fraction and the terminating fraction

\[f_{*} = \frac{A_{*}}{B_{*}} = b_{*} + \frac{a_{1}}{b_{1} + }\frac{a_{2}}{b_{2} + }\cdots\frac{a_{n}}{b_{n}}\]

is called the _n_th convergent of \(f\).

If \(\lim\limits_{n\to n}\frac{A_{*}}{B_{*}}\) exists, the infinite continued fraction \(f\) is said to be convergent. If \(a_{i}\) = 1 and the \(b_{i}\) are integers there is always convergence.

**Theorems**

(1) If \(a_{i}\) and \(b_{i}\) are positive then \(f_{2n} < < _{2n + 2}\), \(f_{2n - 1} > f_{2n + 1}\).

(2) If \(f_{n}\) = \(\frac{A_{*}}{B_{*}}\)

\[\begin{array}{l} A_{*} = b_{n}A_{*n - 1} + a_{n}A_{n - 2} \\ B_{n} = b_{n}B_{n - 1} + a_{n}B_{n - 2} \\ \end{array}\]

where \(A_{i}\) = 1, \(A_{i}\) = \(b_{i}\), \(B_{i}\) = 0, \(B_{i}\) = 1.

